{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2","c3","c4"],"primary":"c3","title":"Prohibition of Anti-Competitive Automated Rent Price-Fixing Ordinance","requirement":"No one may sell, license or otherwise provide an algorithmic device to a landlord, and landlords may not use one to set rental rates or occupancy levels for residential rental property. An algorithmic device is software that uses algorithms on nonpublic competitor data to advise or recommend rental rates or occupancy levels. Tenants may sue for injunctive relief, damages, or civil penalties of up to $1,000 per violation, and a prevailing tenant recovers costs and reasonable attorney's fees.","key_value":"Civil penalties up to $1,000 per violation; each month of use and each affected rental property is a separate violation","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"San Diego Municipal Code §§ 98.1101–98.1104","coverage_conditions":"All residential rental property in the City of San Diego. Applies to persons who sell, license or provide algorithmic devices and to landlords who use them. Lease provisions limiting recovery of attorney's fees are unenforceable, and remedies are cumulative.","exemptions":"Excludes software or products used to publish reports on rental rates or occupancy levels from either aggregated historical nonpublic competitor data more than 90 days old or publicly available information, where the reports do not recommend rates or occupancy for future leases or renewals. Also excludes software used to set rental rates or income limits under local, state or federal affordable housing program guidelines.","conflict_note":"Status conflict: c3 (D076, official) presents the text as a proposed ordinance with status 'pending' and no effective date. c1 (D002), c2 (D037) and c4 (D086) report the ban as already enacted and in force. c1 and c2 give an effective date of '2025-06' (month only). The official document is the proposed draft and does not show that the ordinance stayed pending, while three later sources independently describe it as in force. Status is therefore set to in_force. effective_date is left null because the sources give only a month (June 2025), the day is not supported, and no official adoption record was provided. This should be verified against the adopted ordinance."}],"dropped":[]}
